      "report_date": "2026-11-25",
      "title": "Analysis of AMOS and a backdoor targeting macOS, with ClickFix as the starting point",
      "summary": "ClickFix lures on compromised Japanese university WordPress sites led an NICT employee's Mac to run a pasted terminal command, which installed AMOS-style infostealer components and two persistent backdoors. The stager, a zsh script, decrypts and runs a payload that steals passwords, browser data, Cookies, SSH keys and keychains within about two minutes. The backdoors use fixed C2 servers, Telegram and DNS dead drop resolvers, and a DGA as fallback, and one of them swaps cryptocurrency addresses in the clipboard, with roughly 150,000 USD in associated deposits observed. The compromised site also loaded a fake verification script and an EtherHiding loader that changes its payload domains often, while a related iOS Safari exploit kit resembled Google's Coruna. Infrastructure analysis suggests infections spread across many countries, including Japan, and are ongoing. Defenders should look for suspicious LaunchDaemons and hidden Application Support folders, watch for DGA lookups and _cfg TXT queries, and check WordPress plugin folders. Users should be warned never to paste commands from web pages into Terminal, and any infected system should be rebuilt with all credentials rotated.",
      "completeness": "High",
